s produced products prior to  release for distribution the purpose of  product testing is to provide patients  with the assurance that the medicine is  potent and pure and safe for use  analytical testing also assures that the  contents match the label claim that  there is batch to batch consistency and  the product is potent impure throughout  shelf-life each state and country has  its own set of testing requirements and  regulations for analytical laboratories  with these requirements come challenges  for both the regulators as well as the  regulated industry so if by way of  learning objective today I would expect  that those that are listening will learn  how medical cannabis products are tested  and the quality standards they will also  learn about the regulatory testing  requirements in the US and other  countries and finally will appreciate  the various testing challenges as state  programs grow and evolve  so to get into the meat of the talk I'd  like to first begin by saying what is  the purpose of testing to some it's  self-evident but for most important  points are the fact that we want to  assure of course the products are  protein pure we want to make sure that  the products match the information on  the label claim this is the message to  the patient and to the distributors and  them in dispensaries about what is the  contents of this product we want to  assure consistency from batch to batch  many of these products are used  chronically patients need to have the  absolute assurance that from batch to  batch a lot a lot container to container  are getting the same product and of  course the ever-important potency and  purity throughout shelf-life  so what are some of these general  testing requirements and quality control  essentially 27 out of the 33 states that  have medical programs require QC testing  prior to product release to the  dispensary these laboratories that  perform the testing must be independent  of the grower processor dispensary or by  by any licensed authority to participate  in a state program many states require  that the independent laboratory be  accredited by a third-party accrediting  body quality control begins with  standardization and what this means is  that you don't test quality into a  product it begins with the step 1 which  in this case is the planting of the seed  or the clone and this standardization  precedes all the way through to harvest  to packaging processing and eventually  to the dispensaries  I can't emphasize enough that the  requirements for standardization be be  something that is engineered to through  the entire process as far as testing for  standards we apply the Good  Manufacturing Practices good agriculture  practices and of course has the when  food production is involved and last but  not least within the laboratory itself  quality assurance program and up-to-date  and complete and form FPS will ensure  consistency within the lab with between  operators and day-to-day consistency  so a few definitions that will pertinent  to the rest of the talk obviously  there's many many more definitions but  these two are particularly important to  me what is an approved laboratory  well the approved laboratory is one that  has a facility that has applied for and  received approval whether it be a  approval or just a registration of  license whatever the state requirements  demand and this approval is received to  identify collect handle and conduct  tests on samples from both growers or  cultivators and processors and the  second key definition is what is the  scope of accreditation this is a term  that's used by the accrediting body to  detail the approved list of test methods  with full method validation that the lab  has been accredited to perform based on  whatever state requirements are so in  other words the crediting body does not  actually write the method nor do they  judge the met method for fitness they  they assess the method and they make  sure that this is the method that's  going to be used every single time that  a sample is tested at this accredited  lab so what are some general provisions  that states require when a laboratory  wants to start business first of all as  I previously mentioned the lab must be  approved or registered by the state here  in Maryland we register lab it's an  application process some states actually  have a formal approval process the  regulatory authorities such as the state  Commission will post on their website a  list of all approved labs so that  information is available to the general  public and to the licensees laboratory  personnel including the directors should  be trained and educated most states have  very specific educational and training  requirements at the director level as  well as managers and lab agents labs are  subject to the inspection by state  authority and so that the state retains  or respond with the right to come in at  any time whether it's a  regular routine inspection or a  for-cause inspection one thing that  would allow the states to come in and do  an inspection many states require  proficiency testing in other words  blinded samples are prepared and made  available to the lab for them to conduct  tests to compare against unknown  quantity to give the further assurance  that the state that labs know what  they're doing with regulations at the  state level come penalties for violating  procedures these can range from fine to  injunction and loss of license we need  to think about falsifying reports but we  know that this can happen and if it does  it is certainly something that is  actionable by a state authority and  finally some states will collect field  samples from time to time to also check  on label content and also to check for  purity  so after the laboratory has decided that  they want to go into a business in a  state with a program here are some of  the basic procedural approval steps that  most states have obviously these will  vary from state to state but in general  the submitted application will of course  include the the name and the address of  the owner the name of the director of  the laboratory a copy of the certificate  of accreditation or in case the  accreditation is still in process some  states for example like Maryland have a  provisional period in which the lab is  allowed to get up to speed and complete  the accreditation so in those cases it  would be a contract with an accrediting  body that would suffice a complete list  of the SOPs that the lab will prepare  and use and I highlight sampling  protocol because we're going to spend  some time talking about that later on  very important the list of all the  laboratory equipment a complete  description of all the tests that are  going to be performed going back to that  definition of scope of accreditation a  full and complete description of the  quality assurance program chain of  custody and sample tracking in states  that have track and trace systems this  reaches out to the transfer of sample to  and from laboratories from other life  and holders like the cultivator or the  processor and of course the competency  the employee there their knowledge and  experience and of course in some states  a background check is required as well  so testing requirements as you can  imagine vary from state to state and  this is just a generalized scheme of  what are most commonly seen as the  testing requirements in the states that  I have done work in some states for  example require a written contract with  between the licensee the grower or the  processor and the testing lab some  states require that the grower processor  laboratory results are entered into the  electronic tracking system which is  controlled by the state program  the scope of testing for each plant  harvest and each process product lot  might vary some from state to state but  in general this is QC so you see me that  every lot or every batch of product that  sits on a dispenser