ideo surveillance or at minimum  it is performed in as a witness with  another person witnessing the sampling  as I mentioned the sample must be  representative the whole batch or lot so  there's various ways that a  statistically sound sample can be taken  and if you've got a bin and you've got  plant material in it you can use a see  from a sampling device to go down into  the middle of the bend  besides the top the bottom of the bin  and and collect an aggregate sample that  is as representative as it possibly can  be and in states that require stability  testing and quality assurance samples it  is my advice that those samples all be  collected at the same time the QC sample  is taken so the QA sample is put in  retain for a later use if in fact  there's a recall or there's a a patient  adverse event that to a sample can be  retrieved and used to retest to  reconfirm the product student  specification and of course the  stability sample will be stored in a  control room temperature environment  until the interval at which the testing  is required occurs  so challenge number two  granulation versus the beautiful bud  this somewhat overlaps with the sampling  dilemma as I said when you've got a bin  of harvest to dry flour clearly you can  see you can see flower buds you can see  leaves and one of the one of the issues  one of the one of the remedies for this  um is to actually size the batch and  what do I mean by sizing the batch the  plant material can be put through a  grinder or a sizer with Teflon coated or  otherwise inert chopping blades so that  the sticky material in the trichomes  isn't lost to the blade and the result  then is a much more uniform harvest  batch and so sampling then becomes a  much easier challenge and it really does  reduce drastically the result the chance  for sampling bias the dilemma though  comes in in the fact that in the US and  this is an observation of mine and I  believe of many others there is a  general patient consumer preference to  see an intact well but quite honestly as  an antelope chemist I don't understand  that but I guess this is something that  has become tradition and so it is  largely unacceptable in the u.s. to have  samples in the de century that represent  granulated flower material if that is  the finished product that the patient is  interested  and once again sampling of that non  granulated flour but may not be  representative of a whole batch and has  much more of a chance to have a sampling  bias  so um challenge number three and this  one is a difficult one even to discuss  but I think it's important enough that  we spend a few minutes talking about the  hell and the why that first of all the  hell how can you inflate THC results you  know the lab has procedures we've got  state rules in place for the most part  everybody is accredited it should be a  fail-safe system but here are five and  I'm not saying these happen these are  potentials but here are five potential  ways that I have conceived of can be  ways in which you can subtly influence  THC results toward a higher level first  of all something that I call secondary  sampling so the sampling has been done  at the licensee the sample comes back to  the laboratory absent video surveillance  the lab analysts they're preparing the  sample that is going to be ready for the  instrument and so obviously that entire  sample has been collected is not that  not needed and so secondary sampling if  the product is not granulated can be  potentially biased at this point  secondly a grower can pre-select what  they are going to make available to the  independent animal exemplar before they  even arrive I can't say once again that  I have witnesses or that I know that  this practice has taken place but once  again it is a potential thirdly if the  met if the analytical methods lacked  sufficient validation you could  introduce a bias by virtue of a bad  method  set fourthly something that I call  intentional and subtle 