usly what is more desired  so the last and most interesting I think  challenge that we've seen goes back to  an incident that occurred about a year  or so ago and it regarded the test for  total yeast and mold totally euston mold  is a typical test that is performed on  botanicals plant material medicinal  plant material and the test typically is  it's based on the test that is published  in the American urban pharmacopoeia  monograph and that monograph is based on  the standardized methods from the  bacteriological analytical manual and  typically what is recognized even to  this day is what is known as you know  traditional plate counting on auger  plate so that is that that is the  recognized standard at this point in  time but with the advent of PCR testing  PCR standing for polymerase chain  reaction testing it is a very it's a  very quantitative method it's very  precise it's very accurate but it does  have some reservations at this point in  time in my opinion  the test is valid for species specific  organisms such as Salmonella and e-coli  so what does that mean in a total used  mold test basically what you're  measuring is any fungal species so we  know that there are thousands and  thousands of those unless there is a  primer which is the equivalent of a  standard for these species the PCR test  in my opinion at this point in time is  not completely valid thirdly the PCR has  the potential to measure both live and  dead DNA of the organism and that can  influence the accuracy of the result and  at this point in time the baam the  biological analytical man manual does  not consider as a valid test for either  total yeast mold or total aerobic counts  so an interesting study was conducted in  Nevada this goes back I believe in 1900  2017 and they were having issues and  these issues were coming to the  attention of the state by various labs  so the state decided to conduct a very  interesting study and in this study the  state collected 41 sample of cannabis