ts in the Netherlands once  again are what you would consider  standards potency water content loss on  drying heavy metals pesticide residue  absent microbiological contaminants and  also appearance the one thing I will add  to about requirements in the Netherlands  is that potency has very tight  specifications around of the label claim  so in other words if if the dried flower  is the finished product and the dry  flower is purported to have ten percent  THC the specifications in the  Netherlands are plus or minus 10 percent  label claims so a range around that 10  would be of course 9 to 11 percent and  those those strict criteria are what  would be equivalent to what you would  see was it with a approved drug product  and so you can see they're very specific  very strict and part of the reason for  this is the years and years of  experience of growing in a standardized  way and perfecting analytical techniques  that they are able to impose these kind  of strict specifications and and I would  say that this is a direction that all  testing programs should be headed  towards some day  moving on to my final country which is  Australia obviously there are many many  more countries that have national laws  and testing requirements but I wanted to  just highlight Australia because they  have a program that I linked off the  ground and growing very rapidly and  under the Therapeutic Goods  Administration therapeutic good order 93  requires once again that each badge each  lot of product is tested and this  includes product that is exported to  exported to other countries and also  product that is imported from other  countries nothing sits on a dispensary  shelf or a pharmacy shelf in Australia  that hasn't been tested and released so  what does Australia rely on as far as  standard basically what they have done  is to adopt Parrs portions of the  European pharmacopoeia just like we have  in the u.s. with the HP monograph for  standardized procedures for things like  