y  by any licensed authority to participate  in a state program many states require  that the independent laboratory be  accredited by a third-party accrediting  body quality control begins with  standardization and what this means is  that you don't test quality into a  product it begins with the step 1 which  in this case is the planting of the seed  or the clone and this standardization  precedes all the way through to harvest  to packaging processing and eventually  to the dispensaries  I can't emphasize enough that the  requirements for standardization be be  something that is engineered to through  the entire process as far as testing for  standards we apply the Good  Manufacturing Practices good agriculture  practices and of course has the when  food production is involved and last but  not least within the laboratory itself  quality assurance program and up-to-date  and complete and form FPS will ensure  consistency within the lab with between  operators and day-to-day consistency  so a few definitions that will pertinent  to the rest of the talk obviously  there's many many more definitions but  these two are particularly important to  me what is an approved laboratory  well the approved laboratory is one that  has a facility that has applied for and  received approval whether it be a  approval or just a registration of  license whatever the state requirements  demand and this approval is received to  identify collect handle and conduct  tests on samples from both growers or  cultivators and processors and the  second key definition is what is the  scope of accreditation this is a term  that's used by the accrediting body to  detail the approved list of test methods  with full method validation that the lab  has been accredited to perform based on  whatever state requirements are so in  other words the crediting body does not  actually write the method nor do they  judge the met method for fitness they  they assess the method and they make  sure that this is the method that's  going to be used every single time that  a sample is tested at this accredited  lab so what are some general provisions  that states require when a laboratory  wants to start business first of all as  I previously mentioned the lab must be  approved or registered by the state here  in Maryland we register lab it's an  application process some states actually  have a formal approval process the  regulatory authorities such as the state  Commission will post on their website a  list of all approved labs so that  information is available to the general  public and to the licensees laboratory  personnel including the directors should  be trained and educated most states have  very specific educational and training  requirements at the director level as  well as managers and lab agents labs are  subject to the inspection by state  authority and so that the state retains  or respond with the right to come in at  any time whether it's a  regular routine inspection or a  for-cause inspection one thing that  would allow the states to come in and do  an inspection many states require  proficiency testing in other words  blinded samples are prepared and made  available to the lab for them to conduct  tests to compare against unknown  quantity to give the further assurance  that the state that labs know what  they're doing with regulations at the  state level come penalties for violating  procedures these can range from fine to  injunction and loss of license we need  to think about falsifying reports but we  know that this can happen and if it does  it is certainly something that is  actionable by a state authority and  finally some states will collect field  samples from time to time to also check  on label content and also to check for  purity  so after the laboratory has decided that  they want to go into a business in a  state with a program here are some of  the basic procedural approval steps that  most states have obviously these will  vary from state to state but in general  the submitted application will of course  include the the name and the address of  the owner the name of the director of  the laboratory a copy of the certificate  of accreditation or in case the  accreditation is still in process some  states for example like Maryland have a  provisional period in which the lab is  allowed to get up to speed and complete  the accreditation so in those cases it  would be a contract with an accrediting  body that would suffice a complete list  of the SOPs that the lab will prepare  and use and I highlight sampling  protocol because we're going to spend  some time talking about that later on  very important the list of all the  laboratory equipment a complete  description of all the tests that are  going to be performed going back to that  definition of scope of accreditation a  full and complete description of the  quality assurance program chain of  custody and sample tracking in states  that have track and trace systems this  reaches out to the transfer of sample to  and from laboratories from other life  and holders like the cultivator or the  processor and of course the competency  the employee there their knowledge and  experience and of course in some states  a background check is required as well  so testing requirements as you can  imagine vary from state to state and  this is just a generalized scheme of  what are most commonly seen as the  testing requirements in the states that  I have done work in some states for  example require a written contract with  between the licensee the grower or the  processor and the testing lab some  states require that the grower processor  laboratory results are entered into the  electronic tracking system which is  controlled by the state program  the scope of testing for each plant  harvest and each process product lot  might vary some from state to state but  in general this is QC so you see me that  every lot or every batch of product that  sits on a dispenser shelf has been  tested and released and reads these  specifications so at minimum we have a  list here which are azam of course some  of the purity requirements pesticide  residue if in fact pesticides have been  used in the cultivation of the plant  solvent residues if that is part of the  extraction process of the cannabinoids  and tripping form the plant material  water activity and moisture content  these are vital to make sure that the  plant are the dried flower if that  finished product has adequate shelf-life  and that there isn't enough water that  would support on microbial growth  microbial contaminants such as  pathological bacteria and fungus  aflatoxin and then on the potency side  of course the two main cannabinoid THC  and CBD and most other states expand  that list to include some of the minor  cannabinoids such as the acid forms of  THC and CBD and also cbn and CBC and  some of the others that would have to be  something that would check be checked on  state by state and their various rule  and then terpenes there's a lot of  variability about testing requirements  or terpenes for example in Maryland we  have a list of eight that we require the  labs to test for if in fact they are on  the label and that's that's an important  component if the labeling does not  include a description of terpene by  content or if the levels are below the  level of detection then the requirement  for testing terpene is waived in some  states and this is a very big one an  important one for me is that some states  require stability testing I think this  is an area that we're going to see grow  Maryland does requirement require it now  as of this year they have not been  enforcing that requirement but it is my  understanding  that they will begin to enforce it and  the significance of stability testing is  that a base of data will be built it  will be important not only for the  grower or processor but also for the  state agencies and and actually for the  world at large because there is quite a  dearth of actual shelf life data to  understand how long these cannabinoids  and terpenoids  to the plant product or the finished  product is process  so shifting gears now to the next the  second half of the talk I'd like to talk  about some of the challenges as you can  understand with every program  there's the ideal which I've just gone  through in the first 10 slides but then  there's also reality things are going to  happen both on the side of the  regulators and the side of the regulated  industry and so what I have in the next  few slides is four examples of what I  witnessed and observed as current  challenges in this space we're going to  spend a little time talking about  sampling of a very important topic and  very timely the next challenge is  something I call granulation versus  beautiful bud and I will explain that  inflating THC test result sadly this is  an issue and then finally  misrepresenting total yeast and mold  test results by virtue of using  different analytical techniques for  measurement so let's move to sampling a  sound sampling plan is absolutely vital  to make sure and to assure the best  possible accuracy in the result and that  that result reflects and represents the  whole batch so in other words these this  is a plant this is a plant that's  harvested there will be plant parts that  will be visible they'll be trim they'll  be leaves they'll be sugar leaves  they'll be families and of course  they'll be of the dried flower bud and  all of this is very non-homogeneous and  so collecting a sample of this sort is  difficult and it's difficult to  represent it statistically sound so what  most states have done and I believe  Maryland was the first to recall this is  that the sample not be collected by the  licensee but rather a representative  from the testing lab who is independent  and unbiased will come in and perform  the test perform the sampling excuse me  sometimes the sampling is performed  under video surveillance or at minimum  it is performed in as a witness with  another person witnessing the sampling  as I mentioned the sample must be  representative the whole batch or lot so  there's various ways that a  statistically sound sample can be taken  and if you've got a bin and you've got  plant material in it you can use a see  from a sampling device to go down into  the middle of the bend  besides the top the bottom of the bin  and and collect an aggregate sample that  is as representative as it possibly can  be and in states that require stability  testing and quality assurance samples it  is my advice that those samples all be  collected at the same time the QC sample  is taken so the QA sample is put in  retain for a later use if in fact  there's a recall or there's a a patient  adverse event that to a sample can be  retrieved and used to retest to  reconfirm the product student  specification and of course the  stability sample will be stored in a  control room temperature environment  until the interval at which the testing  is required occurs  so challenge number two  granulation versus the beautiful bud  this somewhat overlaps with the sampling  dilemma as I said when you've got a bin  of harvest to dry flour clearly you can  see you can see flower buds you can see  leaves and one of the one of the issues  one of the one of the remedies for this  um is to actually size the batch and  what do I mean by sizing the batch the  plant material can be put through a  grinder or a sizer with Teflon coated or  otherwise inert chopping blades so that  the sticky material in the trichomes  isn't lost to the blade and the result  then is a much more uniform harvest  batch and so sampling then becomes a  much easier challenge and it really does  reduce drastically the result the chance  for sampling bias the dilemma though  comes in in the fact that in the US and  this is an observation of mine and I  believe of many others there is a  general patient consumer preference to  see an intact well but quite honestly as  an antelope chemist I don't understand  that but I guess this is something that  has become tradition and so it is  largely unacceptable in the u.s. to have  samples in the de century that represent  granulated flower material if that is  the finished product that the patient is  interested  and once again sampling of that non  granulated flour but may not be  representative of a whole batch and has  much more of a chance to have a sampling  bias  so um challenge number three and this  one is a difficult one even to discuss  but I think it's important enough that  we spend a few minutes talking about the  hell and the why that first of all the  hell how can you inflate THC results you  know the lab has procedures we've got  state rules in place for the most part  everybody is accredited it should be a  fail-safe system but here are five and  I'm not saying these happen these are  potentials but here are five potential  ways that I have conceived of can be  ways in which you can subtly influence  THC results toward a higher level first  of all something that I call secondary  sampling so the sampling has been done  at the licensee the sample comes back to  the laboratory absent video surveillance  the lab analysts they're preparing the  sample that is going to be ready for the  instrument and so obviously that entire  sample has been collected is not that  not needed and so secondary sampling if  the product is not granulated can be  potentially biased at this point  secondly a grower can pre-select what  they are going to make available to the  independent animal exemplar before they  even arrive I can't say once again that  I have witnesses or that I know that  this practice has taken place but once  again it is a potential thirdly if the  met if the analytical methods lacked  sufficient validation you could  introduce a bias by virtue of a bad  method  set fourthly something that I call  intentional and subtle delusion errors  what does this need well obviously  instruments are very very sensitive and  so the sample from preparation to  dilution in order to have the proper  concentration for detection and  quantitation will be diluted possibly  several times before it's actually  introduced into the equipment during  that process subtle dilution errors  which could add up could potentially  concentrate the product that is going to  be ejected in the machine and thus you  know an unintentional or intentional  inflation of the TT results could occur  and last and probably most egregious and  I hope I would never experience this but  if data after the analysis is completed  is exported as a text file and is  unprotected from tampering then results  through human intervention could be  changed so the second question then is  why does this occur why why would there  be reason to ever want to inflate T's  the results well the two most  predictable answers that I can think of  our things that I've seen in Maryland  once again and probably in other states  as well and that is for the most part  pricing of the product if it's not  controlled by state rule typically is  based on THC content so the grower sells  to the processor that price from the  grower the processor will be higher  based on the higher level of THC  likewise from the processor to the  dispensary and then ultimately from the  dispensary to the patient and so there  is incentive throughout the entire chain  to  want to present the product with the  maximum amount of THC possible secondly  and I know this is happening in States  especially with states that have rules  about approved conditions that might  allow for some more flexibility in  physician recommendation we have seen  and I have to admit I don't like it but  that there is a recreational component  to medical market and so if you believe  that and if you witness that then it's  understandable that in the recreational  space the higher THC content is  obviously what is more desired  so the last and most interesting I think  challenge that we've seen goes back to  an incident that occurred about a year  or so ago and it regarded the test for  total yeast and mold totally euston mold  is a typical test that is performed on  botanicals plant material medicinal  plant material and the test typically is  it's based on the test that is published  in the American urban pharmacopoeia  monograph and that monograph is based on  the standardized methods from the  bacteriological analytical manual and  typically what is recognized even to  this day is what is known as you know  traditional plate counting on auger  plate so that is that that is the  recognized standard at this point in  time but with the advent of PCR testing  PCR standing for polymerase chain  reaction testing it is a very it's a  very quantitative method it's very  precise it's very accurate but it does  have some reservations at this point in  time in my opinion  the test is valid for species specific  organisms such as Salmonella and e-coli  so what does that mean in a total used  mold test basically what you're  measuring is any fungal species so we  know that there are thousands and  thousands of those unless there is a  primer which is the equivalent of a  standard for these species the PCR test  in my opinion at this point in time is  not completely valid thirdly the PCR has  the potential to measure both live and  dead DNA of the organism and that can  influence the accuracy of the result and  at this point in time the baam the  biological analytical man manual does  not consider as a valid test for either  total yeast mold or total aerobic counts  so an interesting study was conducted in  Nevada this goes back I believe in 1900  2017 and they were having issues and  these issues were coming to the  attention of the state by various labs  so the state decided to conduct a very  interesting study and in this study the  state collected 41 sample of cannabis  flour these samples were subjected to  testing by both the traditional plating  method as I previously described as well  as the PCR technique  specification which is generally  recognized and is included in the HP  monograph is a respective occasion of  less than 10,000 colony forming units so  the samples were prepared for plating  based on the baam method and for the PCR  technique of the samples were prepared  based on manufacturer's directions  so the results that came out of this was  very interesting 16 of the 41 samples  passed by both techniques but more  importantly 20 of the 41 so basically  half tailed by plating but then most  surprisingly three of the 41 only three  of the 41 failed by the PCR test so what  did the state conclude from this study  basically that the PCR technique  generated on the average of a result of  an 85 percent false positive rate and  that the PCR test therefore was not  considered valid and as a consequence  the state of nevada has now banned the  PCR as a technique for measuring either  total yeast enrolled or total Rove  account until such time as the mecca can  be fully validated by a recognized  authorities such as the ASAC or ASTM or  or some other testing organizations that  performs this kind of work and another  conclusion that came out of this test  from nevada was that state said came on  board downstream such as california took  a look at this and said well you know  what's really important it is what is  pathogenic to humans and so California  opted to drop the test altogether in  favor of species specific pathogenic  tests with limits such as we mentioned  like Salmonella and e-coli so that that  really was a very important study and I  believe that other shades come on board  they will be taking a look at this and  at some point in time I would hope to  see the PCR test become the recognized  standard as we advanced science  so now for the remaining time and the  remaining slides that I have I'm going  to shift 